From: Lars Karlslund <lars@karlslund.dk>
Subject: [PATCH] remoteproc: qcom_q6v5_pas: detect EL2 ownership under nVHE too

is_kernel_in_hyp_mode() is only true when the kernel itself runs at EL2
(VHE). Booted at EL2 without a hypervisor but with id_aa64mmfr1.vh=0, the
kernel runs at EL1 with the nVHE KVM hypervisor at EL2: PAS is still not
usable for the DSPs started by the boot chain, but the driver took the PAS
path and left the ADSP/CDSP unattached. The EL2 overlay (x1-el2.dtso) is
the only source of qcom,broken-reset, so treat it as EL2 ownership too.
---
--- a/drivers/remoteproc/qcom_q6v5_pas.c	2026-10-21 11:39:55.782573670 +0200
+++ b/drivers/remoteproc/qcom_q6v5_pas.c	2026-10-21 11:40:23.922249610 +0200
@@ -883,7 +883,7 @@
 	const char *fw_name, *dtb_fw_name = NULL;
 	const struct rproc_ops *ops = &qcom_pas_ops;
 	int ret;
-	bool in_el2 = is_kernel_in_hyp_mode();
+	bool in_el2;
 	bool el2_pas_capable = qcom_pas_is_el2_pas_capable();
 	bool use_pas;
 	bool pas_avail, scm_avail, broken_reset;
@@ -895,6 +895,12 @@
 	pas_avail = qcom_pas_is_available();
 	scm_avail = qcom_scm_is_available();
 	broken_reset = device_property_read_bool(&pdev->dev, "qcom,broken-reset");
+	/*
+	 * Only the EL2 overlay (no hypervisor) marks the DSPs qcom,broken-reset.
+	 * Under nVHE (id_aa64mmfr1.vh=0) Linux still owns EL2 but runs at EL1,
+	 * where is_kernel_in_hyp_mode() is false.
+	 */
+	in_el2 = is_kernel_in_hyp_mode() || broken_reset;
 
 	/* PAS is usable at EL2 only on platforms whose TZ firmware supports it. */
 	use_pas = pas_avail && scm_avail && (!in_el2 || el2_pas_capable);
